I have thoroughly mapped the owner-identity implementation. Here is the complete report.

## Overview & package layout

This is a Spring PetClinic REST app whose owner-creation logic lives entirely in an **OfficeFloor function pipeline** (there is no Spring `@RestController` arm — the only code that generates `customerCode`/`membershipNumber` is the function below). The pipeline is wired declaratively.

Key directories:
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/` — the pipeline step classes (one class per step)
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/` — shared derivation helpers (`Luhn`, `CityRegion`, `PostcodeRange`, `MembershipLevel`, `IdentityKey`, `ContactPreference`)
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` — MapStruct mapper that derives read-only DTO fields (`checkDigit`, `locality`, `membershipLevel`, `identityKey`, etc.)
- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` — JPA entity (persisted fields)
- `src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` — pipeline wiring (step order)
- `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` — DTO schema + field descriptions (drives generated DTOs)
- `src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/` — the acceptance tests

The **pipeline order** (from `owners.POST.yml`) for the create path is: `validate → normalizeTelephone → normalizeEmail → validatePostcode → ensureUniqueIdentity → ensureCityCapacity → resolveRegistrationDate → ensureDailyLimit → build → applyRegistrationDate → assignCustomerCode → assignMembershipNumber → assignHousehold → assignHouseholdSize → countNamesakes → save → audit → respond`.

---

## 1. customerCode — generation & current format

**File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`** (the sole generator)

```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
    long ownersInCity = Cities.countIn(ownerRepository, owner.getCity());
    owner.setCustomerCode(customerCode(owner.getCity(), owner.getLastName(), ownersInCity + 1));
}

private static String customerCode(String city, String lastName, long sequence) {
    return String.format("%s-%s-%04d", prefix(city), prefix(lastName), sequence);
}

/** Upper-cased first three letters of the value, used as a code segment. */
private static String prefix(String value) {
    return value.substring(0, Math.min(3, value.length())).toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);
}
```

Current format (per the actual code): `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`, e.g. `LON-SMI-0007`. `NNNN` is a **per-city** 4-digit zero-padded sequence = `(owners already in that city) + 1`.

Note the sequence source is `Cities.countIn(...)` — **File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/Cities.java`** (`countIn` iterates `repository.findAll()` and counts case-insensitive city matches; also used by `EnsureCityCapacity`).

Documentation drift to be aware of: the `owners.POST.yml` comment and the `openapi.yml` description (line 2096-2100, example `FRA-0007`) both describe an OLDER `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` **global** sequence format — neither matches the current `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` per-city code.

The **target format** the acceptance test now demands is `<REGION>-<HASH8>` — see section 8.

---

## 2. membership number & check digit

**Membership number — File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignMembershipNumber.java`**

```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
    String yy = String.format("%02d", owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100);
    owner.setMembershipNumber(owner.getCustomerCode() + "-M" + yy);
}
```

Format `<customerCode>-M<YY>`, e.g. `LON-SMI-0007-M26`. It runs after `AssignCustomerCode` and `ApplyRegistrationDate`, so it depends directly on the customerCode string and the adjusted registration-date year. (OpenAPI desc at line 2102-2106.)

**Check digit — File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/Luhn.java`**

```java
public static int checkDigit(String value) {
    int sum = 0;
    boolean doubleDigit = true;
    for (int i = value.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        char c = value.charAt(i);
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
            continue;
        }
        int d = c - '0';
        if (doubleDigit) {
            d *= 2;
            if (d > 9) {
                d -= 9;
            }
        }
        sum += d;
        doubleDigit = !doubleDigit;
    }
    return (10 - (sum % 10)) % 10;
}
```

The check digit is computed over the digits of `customerCode` and exposed only on the DTO via the mapper (not persisted). **File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`**:

```java
@Mapping(target = "checkDigit", expression = "java(checkDigit(owner))")
...
default Integer checkDigit(Owner owner) {
    return Luhn.checkDigit(owner.getCustomerCode());
}
```

Important dependency: changing customerCode to `<REGION>-<HASH8>` changes which digits Luhn sees (hex letters are ignored by `Luhn.checkDigit`, only 0-9 count). OpenAPI desc at line 2108-2116.

---

## 3. "create audit record" logic for owners

**File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`**

```java
